<!--
    kuna `tailform`: at the end of a function `if (c) { S } return K;` and
    `if (!c) return K; S; return K;` are the same program, and the binary's
    return layout, not the source, decides which one structuring recovers.
    The option re-spells each function-tail `if` by the printed size of its
    conditional part: wrapped up to 4 lines, a guard clause above.

    Stage / sub-stage: P8 "goto-quality", a print-tree edit after final
    structuring.  The P0 assertion `option tailform off|on|<n>|<void>,<value>`
    sets the size threshold.

    The bytes are .text of decompiler/crates/kuna-analysis/tests/fixtures/
    tailform.c built by gcc 11.4 at -O2 (tailform_gcc_O2_x86_64):
    note_if_zero (`if (a == 0) note();`, a tail call with its own exit),
    record_pair (`if (a) { record(a); record(b); } return 0;`, whose last
    block holds the calls and the return), record_above (a compound condition
    whose arm repeats the final `return 0;`), replay (a long void body that
    shares the epilogue) and first_value (`return -1` and `return 0`, the
    control).

    Pass 1 runs with `option tailform off` and prints the binary's form.
    Pass 2 (`option tailform on`, the default) wraps the short tails, drops
    the repeated return and guards the long body; first_value is the same.
-->
